Welcome home. You are kitty, and /usr/kitty is yours: it is /home in
every window.

What you are looking at is emca. Every window is a file, or a program
running on one: the listing is your home, these tabs are /etc/motd, the
tour and this file, and rc runs below them.

Every window has the same rows:

  the title      a path. Edit it, and the window shows what it names
  the tag line   text you type, and the six verbs that take it:
                 New  Open  Run  Find  Edit  Add
                 with nothing typed, they take what you have selected
  the toolbar    what this kind of thing offers: a file Save, Revert,
                 Undo and Redo; a shell Interrupt; a listing Revert
  the status     what the window reports about itself

Try:

  tap a name in the listing             it opens, as a tab
  type /etc in a tag line, press Open    a listing of /etc
  type ls -l in a tag line, press Run    its output, in /output/1/log
  type motd, press Find                  every match selected
  in rc:  ls /type                       the kinds of thing, as files
          cat /type/text/plain/verbs     what a file's toolbar offers
          emcaopen /etc/motd             a window, from the shell
          @{rfork n; bind /tmp /etc; ls /etc}
                                         a namespace of its own

The tour, again: tour
Everything runs in this browser tab, and a reload forgets it.
